Returns depend on how many selections win and their odds. A single winner at 5/1 with a 10p stake returns 60p on that one single bet, but the total stake was £6.30. Multiple winners at higher odds generate profit across singles, doubles, trebles, and accumulator combinations. The calculator sums all winning returns to show the total payout.
## Each Way Lucky 63 Explained
Each way betting transforms a Lucky 63 into a 126-bet wager by placing two separate bets on each selection: one for the win and one for a place finish. This doubles the total stake, as every bet within the Lucky 63 structure is duplicated. Place bets typically pay at a fraction of the win odds, commonly one-quarter or one-fifth, depending on the event terms. This approach provides greater coverage but requires careful consideration of the increased cost.
### How Each Way Betting Works
An each way bet consists of two equal stakes. The win portion pays out if the selection finishes first, while the place portion pays if the selection finishes within the specified place positions, usually determined by the number of runners and race type. In horse racing, place terms vary: races with five to seven runners may offer places for the top three at one-quarter odds, while larger fields might extend to four places at one-fifth odds.
### Cost Impact on Lucky 63
When applying each way terms to a Lucky 63, the 63 standard bets are matched by 63 place bets, creating 126 total bets. A £1 per bet Lucky 63 each way costs £126, as opposed to £63 for a win-only Lucky 63. The potential return increases, but so does the required stake. Players must weigh the benefit of place coverage against the doubled outlay, particularly when backing selections at longer odds where place returns may be modest.
| Bet Structure | Number of Bets | Total Stake at £1 |
|---------------|----------------|-------------------|
| Lucky 63 (win only) | 63 | £63 |
| Lucky 63 each way | 126 | £126 |
The lucky 63 calculator can factor in each way terms to project both win and place returns, helping assess whether the accumulator structure justifies the additional stake and potential profit.
## Lucky 63 vs Heinz and Other Accumulator Bets
The Lucky 63 sits within a family of accumulator bets that vary by the number of selections and bet types included. A Lucky 63 uses six selections to create 63 bets, including all singles, doubles, trebles, and higher accumulators. The Heinz also uses six selections but excludes singles, producing 57 bets. The Lucky 15 requires four selections for 15 bets, while the Super Heinz uses seven selections for 120 bets. Understanding these differences helps players choose the structure that matches their confidence level and budget.
### Lucky 63 vs Lucky 15
The Lucky 15 covers four selections across 15 bets: four singles, six doubles, four trebles, and one four-fold accumulator. This lower-cost option suits players with fewer confident picks. The Lucky 63 extends coverage to six selections, creating 63 bets with substantially higher stake requirements. Both include singles, providing a safety net if only one selection wins. The Lucky 63 offers greater return potential when multiple selections succeed, but the Lucky 15 limits exposure when fewer winners materialise. Choice depends on the number of selections a player trusts and their willingness to increase the stake for broader coverage.
### Lucky 63 vs Heinz
Both the Lucky 63 and Heinz use six selections, but the Heinz excludes the six singles, reducing the total to 57 bets. This makes the Heinz cheaper at the same unit stake—£57 versus £63 at £1 per bet. However, if only one selection wins, the Lucky 63 returns the odds on that single, while the Heinz returns nothing. The Heinz suits players confident that multiple selections will win, as the omission of singles lowers cost without sacrificing return on doubles and higher accumulators. The Lucky 63 provides a safety buffer through singles coverage, trading a modest stake increase for protection against isolated winners.
| Bet Type | Selections | Number of Bets | Includes Singles |
|----------|------------|----------------|------------------|
| Lucky 15 | 4 | 15 | Yes |
| Heinz | 6 | 57 | No |
| Lucky 63 | 6 | 63 | Yes |
| Super Heinz | 7 | 120 | No |
## Are Lucky 63 Bets Worth It?
A Lucky 63 bet can be worthwhile when a bettor has confidence in multiple selections at reasonable odds, but the 63-unit stake requirement demands careful consideration. This bet type offers profit potential even when not all selections win, thanks to its coverage through singles, doubles, and trebles, making it suitable for players with sufficient bankroll and strategic intent.
The Lucky 63 structure contains 6 singles, 15 doubles, 20 trebles, 6 four-folds, one five-fold, and one six-fold accumulator. This coverage means a return is possible with just one winner, though profit typically requires at least two or three successful selections at decent odds. Each way options can further enhance returns on horse racing or sports with place terms.
Lucky 63 bets work best when selections have odds above evens and the bettor expects four or more to win. In such scenarios, the stake spreads risk while maintaining accumulator upside. However, they become less suitable for pure long-shot accumulators where multiple winners are unlikely, or for bettors with limited bankrolls who cannot comfortably afford 63 units.
Lucky 63 reviews consistently highlight the importance of bankroll management. Players should calculate potential return scenarios before placing stakes, ensuring the total commitment aligns with their betting budget and risk tolerance. The bet type rewards informed selection rather than speculative punting.
